Since your friend reads three times as fast as you, it will take him the reciprocal of 3 times the time it takes you to read the same novella. The reciprocal of 3 is \(\frac{1}{3}\). Therefore, it will take your friend \(\frac{1}{3} \times 120 = 40\) minutes to read the same novella.

\boxed{40}

To find the number that results when 50 is increased by $120\%$, we first need to understand what $120\%$ of 50 is.
The formula to calculate the increase is: \[ \text{Increase} = \text{Original Number} \times \text{Percentage Increase} \] Substituting the given values into the formula: \[ \text{Increase} = 50 \times 120\% \] Since $120\%$ can be written as a fraction, which is $1.2$, we can rewrite the formula as: \[ \text{Increase} = 50 \times 1.2 \] Performing the multiplication: \[ \text{Increase} = 60 \] Therefore, the increase in value is 60. Next, we add this increase to the original number to find the final result: \[ \text{Final Result} = \text{Original Number} + \text{Increase} \] \[ \text{Final Result} = 50 + 60 \] \[ \text{Final Result} = 110 \] Thus, when 50 is increased by $120\%$, the result is 110.
\boxed{110}

