All triangles have the same value, and all circles have the same value. What is the sum of three circles? \begin{align*}
\Delta + \bigcirc + \Delta + \bigcirc + \Delta&= 21\\
\bigcirc + \Delta+\bigcirc+\Delta+\bigcirc &= 19\\
\bigcirc + \bigcirc + \bigcirc &= \ ?
\end{align*}

chains:
We are given two equations: \begin{align*}
\Delta + \bigcirc + \Delta + \bigcirc + \Delta &= 21 \label{eq1}\\
\bigcirc + \Delta + \bigcirc + \Delta + \bigcirc &= 19 \label{eq2}
\end{align*} We can simplify these equations by combining like terms: \begin{align*}
3\Delta + 2\bigcirc &= 21 \label{eq3}\\
3\bigcirc + 2\Delta &= 19 \label{eq4}
\end{align*} Subtracting equation (3) from equation (4) gives us: \begin{align*}
(3\bigcirc + 2\Delta) - (3\Delta + 2\bigcirc) &= 19 - 21 \label{eq5} \\
3\bigcirc - 3\Delta + 2\Delta - 2\bigcirc &= -2 \label{eq6} \\
\bigcirc - \Delta &= -2 \label{eq7}
\end{align*} We can now solve equation (7) for one variable in terms of the other. Let's solve for $\Delta$ in terms of $\bigcirc$: \begin{align*}
\Delta &= \bigcirc + 2 \label{eq8}
\end{align*} Now we can substitute equation (8) into equation (3): \begin{align*}
3(\bigcirc + 2) + 2\bigcirc &= 21 \label{eq9} \\
3\bigcirc + 6 + 2\bigcirc &= 21 \label{eq10} \\
5\bigcirc + 6 &= 21 \label{eq11} \\
5\bigcirc &= 15 \label{eq12} \\
\bigcirc &= 3 \label{eq13}
\end{align*} Finally, we can substitute equation (13) back into equation (8) to find the value of $\Delta$: \begin{align*}
\Delta &= 3 + 2 \label{eq14} \\
\Delta &= 5 \label{eq15}
\end{align*} Now we can find the sum of three circles by adding three times the value of $\bigcirc$: \begin{align*}
3\bigcirc &= 3 \times 3 \label{eq16} \\
3\bigcirc &= 9 \label{eq17}
\end{align*} Therefore, the sum of three circles is \boxed{9}.

