chains:
To evaluate the expression $(2^2)^3$, we can use the power of a power rule of exponents.
The rule states that for any real numbers $a$ and $b$, and any positive integer $n$, $(a^b)^c = a^{bc}$.
Applying this rule to our problem, we have:
\begin{align*}
(2^2)^3 &= 2^{2 \cdot 3} \\
&= 2^6 \\
&= 64
\end{align*}
Therefore, $(2^2)^3 = 64$.
\boxed{64}

