chains:
The graph of $\frac{1}{q(x)}$ has two vertical asymptotes at $x=-1$ and $x=1$. This means that $q(x)$ has factors of $(x+1)$ and $(x-1)$. Since $q(x)$ is a quadratic, it can only have these two factors, so $q(x) = k(x+1)(x-1)$ for some constant $k$. We are also given that $q(2) = 6$, so we can find $k$:
\begin{align*}
q(2) &= k(2+1)(2-1) \\
6 &= k(3)(1) \\
k &= 2
\end{align*}
Therefore, $q(x) = 2(x+1)(x-1)$.
\boxed{2(x+1)(x-1)}
The answer is: 2(x+1)(x-1)

