We need to interpret the problem: "A yogurt shop sells four flavors of yogurt and has six different toppings. How many combinations of one flavor and two different toppings are available?" The number of ways to choose a flavor (4 choices) and a set of two different toppings from six. Order of toppings presumably not matter (combinations), so number of ways to choose 2 toppings from 6 = C(6,2) = 15. Multiply by 4 flavors: 4*15 = 60. So answer is 60. However need to ensure if "combinations of one flavor and two different toppings" implies choose one flavor and choose two toppings (order among toppings does not matter). Yes. So 60.

Thus final answer: \boxed{60}.
