To find the product of the solutions of the equation $-35=-x^2-2x$, we first rewrite the equation in standard quadratic form:

\begin{align*}
-x^2 - 2x + 35 &= 0
\end{align*}

To simplify, we can multiply the entire equation by -1 to make the leading coefficient positive:

\begin{align*}
x^2 + 2x - 35 &= 0
\end{align*}

Next, we need to factor this quadratic equation. We look for two numbers that multiply to -35 and add up to 2. These numbers are 7 and -5. Thus, we can factor the quadratic as:

\begin{align*}
(x + 7)(x - 5) &= 0
\end{align*}

Setting each factor equal to zero gives us the solutions:

\begin{align*}
x + 7 &= 0 \quad \text{or} \quad x - 5 = 0 \\
x &= -7 \quad \text{or} \quad x = 5
\end{align*}

To find the product of the solutions, we multiply the two solutions together:

\begin{align*}
-7 \times 5 &= -35
\end{align*}

Thus, the product of the solutions is -35.

\boxed{-35}

