We need to find triangle with integer side lengths a<b<c (distinct) and a+b+c=20. Max of a side given distinct integers? Let's find max possible c. Want max c such that there exist distinct a,b positive integers with a+b=20-c, and triangle inequality: c < a+b (strict) i.e., c < a+b. Since a+b = 20-c, we need c < 20-c => 2c < 20 => c < 10. So c max integer less than 10 = 9. Also need triangle inequalities for the other two sides: a < b + c (but if c is largest, a<b<c then a < b + c always holds). So condition is just c < a+b i.e., c < 20 - c => c <10. So max c = 9. Need also that a,b distinct and positive, and also a,b < c? Actually we assumed c largest. So a,b distinct integers less than c, sum to 11. Need distinct positive integers a,b with a<b, a+b=11. Options: (1,10), (2,9), (3,8), (4,7), (5,6). All distinct. So works. So max side length is 9. So answer: \boxed{9}.\[
\begin{aligned}
&\text{Let the side lengths be }a<b<c\text{ with }a+b+c=20.\\
&\text{By the triangle inequality, }c<a+b\;\Longrightarrow\;c<20-c\;\Longrightarrow\;2c<20\;\Longrightarrow\;c<10.\\
&\text{Since the sides are integers, the largest possible side length is }c=9.\\
&\text{We also need distinct integers }a,b\text{ with }a+b=20-9=11\text{ and }a<b.\\
&(1,10),\;(2,9),\;(3,8),\;(4,7),\;(5,6)\text{ are all valid.}
\end{aligned}
\]


\boxed{9}

