We are given the functional equation
\begin{equation*} f(x+y) = f(x)f(y) \end{equation*}
for all real numbers $x$ and $y,$ and we are told that $f(2)=3.$ To find $f(6),$ we can set $x=2$ and $y=4,$ so that $x+y=6.$ Then, using the given functional equation, we have: \begin{align*} f(6) &= f(x+y) \\ &= f(x)f(y) \\ &= f(2)f(4) \end{align*} To find $f(4),$ we can set $x=2$ and $y=2,$ so that $x+y=4.$ Then, using the given functional equation again, we have: \begin{align*} f(4) &= f(x+y) \\ &= f(x)f(y) \\ &= f(2)f(2) \end{align*} Since $f(2)=3,$ we have: \begin{align*} f(4) &= 3 \cdot 3 \\ &= 9 \end{align*} Now, substituting $f(4)=9$ back into the equation for $f(6),$ we have: \begin{align*} f(6) &= f(2)f(4) \\ &= 3 \cdot 9 \\ &= 27 \end{align*} Therefore, $f(6)=\boxed{27}.$\boxed{27}

